## DESCRIPTION
##   Estimating directional derivatives
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - multivariable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation of multivariable functions)
## DBsection(Directional derivatives and the gradient)
## Date(10/15/09)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(ApproxDirDeriv)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('4')
## Section1('14.4')
## Problem1('69 70 71 72 73 74')
## TitleText2('Calculus')
## AuthorText2('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText2('5')
## Section2('14')
## Problem2('69 70 71 72 73 74')
## KEYWORDS('Gradient', 'Directional Derivative')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'answerHints.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->flags->set(tolType => 'absolute');

$a = random(0, 1);
$A = $a == 0 ? 'i' : 'j';

$b = random(0, 1);
($BP, $BV) = $b == 0 ? ('(2,3)', 'j') : ('(3,2)', 'i');

$c = random(0, 1);
@C = ("(4,1)", "(1,4)");

$d = random(0, 4);

$ansC =
    Compute("6/sqrt(2)")->cmp(tolerance => "0.3")->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
        Compute("4") => "Try an approximation that reaches the contour z = 10",
        Compute("4/sqrt(2)") =>
        "Try an approximation that reaches the contour z = 10",
        Formula("6") =>
        "If you travel in the direction <1,1>, how far have you traveled (horizontally)?"
    ));

BEGIN_PGML
Use the contour diagram for [`f(x,y)`] shown in the figure to estimate the
directional derivative of [` f`] in the direction [` \vec{v}`] at
the point P. 

>>[!!]{'HGM4-14-4-40-Gradients-etc.png'}{400}<<

a) At the point [` P = (2,2) `] in the direction  [`\vec{v} = \vec{[$A]}`], 
    the directional derivative is approximately
    [_]{Compute("2")->cmp(tolerance=>"0.2")}

b) At the point [` P = [$BP]`] in the direction [` \vec{v} = -\vec{[$BV]} `], 
    the directional derivative is approximately 
    [_]{Compute("-2")->cmp(tolerance=>"0.2")}

c) At the point [` P = [$C[$c]] `] in the direction 
    [`` \vec{v} = \frac{\vec{i} + \vec{j}}{\sqrt{2}} ``], 
    the directional derivative is approximately [_]{$ansC}

d) At the point [` P = ([$d],0) `] in the direction [` \vec{v} = -\vec{i} `], the directional derivative is approximately
[_]{Compute("0")->cmp(tolerance=>"0.2")}

END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();
